    "path": "/faq/code-reference",
    "title": "International Code Reference",
    "description": "A construction code is a legal document that changes by jurisdiction, and a calculator that assumed one would be wrong almost everywhere.",
    "content": "<h1>International Code Reference</h1>\n<nav aria-label=\"Breadcrumb\"><a href=\"/\">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href=\"/faq/\">Questions and Answers</a> &rsaquo; International Code Reference</nav>\n<p>Reference notes on the standards behind each method, with jurisdiction and edition stated.</p>\n<p>Costing and commercial control. 5 questions answered below, each with the arithmetic behind it and the limits of what the tool decides. <a href=\"/code-reference/\">Open the International Code Reference tool</a>.</p>\n<h2>What it is</h2><p>A construction code is a legal document that changes by jurisdiction, and a calculator that assumed one would be wrong almost everywhere. This reference exists to point at the right standard rather than to substitute for it, and to make clear which questions are answered locally.\n\nIt covers the areas that come up in estimating: material standards, structural design bases, electrical and plumbing requirements, fire, thermal and drainage. Each entry names the standard and the jurisdiction it applies to, with the caveat that the edition in force is a local matter. What the reference will not do is tell you a wall is compliant, because compliance is a judgement about a specific design against a specific edition.</p>\n<h2>What it calculates</h2><ul><li>Lists the standards relevant to each construction discipline, with the jurisdiction each applies to.</li><li>Names the body that publishes each standard, so the current edition can be confirmed at source.</li><li>Flags where a design decision depends on a local code rather than on a calculation.</li><li>Keeps jurisdiction explicit, so a standard is never presented as universal.</li><li>Links each reference to the calculators it affects, so a design input can be traced to its basis.</li><li>Reports a discipline with no reference held as a gap rather than an absence of requirements.</li><li>States that the edition in force is confirmed locally, since standards are revised and adopted on different dates.</li><li>Groups references by the design decision they govern, so the standard behind a specific input can be found quickly.</li><li>Keeps a note of where a local requirement replaces a regional one, since that is where imported assumptions fail.</li></ul>\n<h2>A worked example</h2><p>A query about the minimum thickness of a structural slab returns the design standards that govern it and the note that the edition in force depends on where the building is. The reference does not return a thickness, because the thickness comes from a design against that standard. What it does is make sure the designer is looking at the right document rather than at a figure from a calculator that assumed a code the project is not built under. That single check prevents a class of error that no amount of arithmetic care can catch.</p>\n<h2>Why contractors use it</h2><ul><li>Jurisdiction is explicit for every reference.</li><li>The publishing body is named, so the current edition can be checked.</li><li>Design inputs are traced to the standard they depend on.</li><li>A discipline without a reference is reported rather than implied to have none.</li><li>The distinction between a reference and a compliance check is stated.</li></ul>\n<h2>What it leaves out</h2><ul><li>It is not the standard itself and does not reproduce its text.</li><li>It does not certify compliance or interpret a clause.</li><li>It does not track which edition is in force in your area.</li><li>It does not cover every discipline or every country, and it does not resolve a conflict between two documents.</li><li>It does not replace a professional's obligations under local law or the authority having jurisdiction.</li></ul>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>What does the code reference contain?</h3><p>References to the standards relevant to each discipline, with the jurisdiction they apply to and the body that publishes them. It is a map of where the requirements live rather than a copy of them. It also states where a design decision depends on a local code, which is often the most useful thing it does.</p><h3>Is this the standard itself?</h3><p>No, and it does not attempt to. Standards are copyrighted, revised on their own cycles and adopted by jurisdictions at different times, so reproducing one would produce a document that is out of date somewhere and unenforceable everywhere. The reference points you at the source, which is where the current requirements actually are.</p><h3>Why does jurisdiction matter so much?</h3><p>Because a requirement that is mandatory in one country is guidance in another and unknown in a third, and a quantity tool that ignored that would be making a legal claim by accident. Naming the jurisdiction for each reference keeps that visible, so a design decision made in one market is not imported into another along with its assumptions. That is a common and expensive mistake on projects where the designer, the contractor and the building are in three different places.</p><h3>Does the calculator check compliance?</h3><p>No. A calculator checks arithmetic and a code checks a design, and the two are different claims. The reference tells you which standard governs a question. Whether a particular design complies is a judgement that requires the standard's text, the design, and someone qualified to read both. The reference's contribution is to get the right text in front of the right person.</p><h3>How current is it?</h3><p>It is as current as the reference list it holds, and each entry names the body that publishes the standard so the edition in force can be confirmed there. Standards change, and a jurisdiction may adopt an edition years after publication, so the confirmation is a local act rather than something a page can do for you.</p>\n<h2>Keep reading</h2><ul>\n<li><a href=\"/code-reference/\">International Code Reference</a>: run the calculation on your own measurements.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/faq/\">Every construction question FRELUX answers</a>: all 62 answer pages by trade.</li>\n<li><a href=\"/calculators/\">All FRELUX calculators</a>: the full tool list.</li>\n</ul>",
